{
	title: 'Commit notices: each commit a session makes pops up, attributed to its tab',
	spec: "When a bash call creates commits, every client gets a notice per commit: title '<tab> <name> · committed', line '<short hash> <subject>', kind 'commit' in its own colour (terminal and web), key 'commit:<hash>' so it never replaces a session's done/failed notice. Clients watching the attributed session get none (they see the commit in its transcript); no web push. Detection rides on task 8w's observation, adding no git spawn per call: begin learns the repository's HEAD reflog path (git rev-parse --git-path logs/HEAD, joined to the existing --show-toplevel call; worktrees have their own) and its size; finish reads only complete lines appended since. Entries whose message starts with 'commit' (initial, amend, merge), 'revert' or 'cherry-pick' are commits made here; pull, rebase, reset, checkout and fast-forward moves are not announced. One git log --no-walk call, only when such entries exist, fetches subjects and Session trailers. Attribution: a Session trailer naming a session of this home wins; else, among bash calls observing that repository whose start precedes the entry's reflog time (seconds), the latest-started one, so a short git commit beats a long ./test running beside it. Each commit hash is announced once host-wide (concurrent calls see the same lines; remember the last few hundred). A shrunk reflog, missing reflog or non-repository announces nothing. Commits made outside Hal's bash calls are out of scope. Host git calls stay read-only (GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0); reading the reflog file takes no lock.",
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['qm', '8w']
}
